dict(wp_id=1002, type="post", path="blog/signs-of-hearing-loss", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/senales-perdida-de-audicion/",
     date="2026-11-10", title="Signs you hear worse than you think",
     seo_title="Signs of Hearing Loss: When to Get a Hearing Test",
     desc="Turning the TV up, losing track of the conversation in a bar, asking people to repeat themselves: signs you hear worse than you think, and what a hearing test involves.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2024/08/P017_Alta_miniRITE_open_dome_fingerprint-768x564-1.jpg", w=768, h=564, alt="Behind-the-ear hearing aid on a fingerprint"),
     html="""<p>Hearing loss hardly ever arrives all at once. It creeps up little by little and the brain gets used to it, so it’s often noticed late, when the family has been raising their voices for quite a while.</p>
<h2>Signs worth paying attention to</h2>
<ul>
<li>You turn the TV up louder than everyone else in the house.</li>
<li>In a bar or at a family meal you struggle to follow the conversation.</li>
<li>You often ask people to repeat themselves.</li>
<li>People seem to speak quietly or mumble.</li>
<li>You understand people worse on the phone than face to face.</li>
<li>You hear ringing or buzzing when there’s no noise around.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you recognise yourself in two or more, it’s worth having your hearing checked. Just one could be an off day.</p>
<h2>Why it’s better not to wait</h2>
<p>When the ear stops picking up certain sounds for years, the brain finds it harder to interpret them again, and getting used to a hearing aid takes longer. What’s more, the Lancet Commission on dementia lists untreated hearing loss among the risk factors that can be changed.</p>
<p>It weighs on everyday life too: people who don’t hear well often stop going to gatherings and meals because they get tired of not following what’s said.</p>
<h2>What the hearing test involves</h2>
<p>At Óptica Almería we are also hearing aid audiologists. The test starts by looking at the ear canal and eardrum with a video otoscope, on a screen, to rule out a wax plug. Then we do audiometry in a soundproof booth, with tones at different volumes, and a test with words to see how much you understand, which isn’t the same as hearing.</p>
<p>It doesn’t hurt. If you like, <a href="/en/stores/">give us a call</a> and we’ll give you a time. There’s more information on our <a href="/en/hearing-aids/">hearing aids</a> page.</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=1001, type="post", path="blog/choosing-sunglasses", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/gafas-de-sol-que-mirar/",
     date="2026-11-10", title="Sunglasses in Almería: what to check before you buy",
     seo_title="Sunglasses in Almería: What to Check Before You Buy",
     desc="UV filter, the CE mark, filter categories 0 to 4 and when polarised lenses are worth it. What to look for in sunglasses under the Almería sun.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2024/08/Persol_ReflexEdition.jpg", w=945, h=473, alt="Persol Reflex Edition campaign in black and white"),
     html="""<p>In Almería we have sun almost all year round, and our eyes build up exposure just like our skin does. Ultraviolet radiation is behind problems such as <a href="/en/blog/pterygium-surfers-eye/">pterygium, known locally as “palm trees” in the eyes</a>, and over the years it is also linked to cataracts. That’s why good sunglasses look after your health as well as looking good.</p>
<h2>The UV filter and the CE mark</h2>
<p>First of all, they should carry the CE mark and UV 400 or 100% UV protection. Dark glasses without a filter are worse than wearing nothing: behind a dark lens the pupil opens up and lets in more radiation.</p>
<p>How dark they are tells you nothing about how much they protect. There are light lenses with full UV filtering.</p>
<h2>The filter category, from 0 to 4</h2>
<p>It’s marked on the arm or on the label and tells you how much light gets through:</p>
<ul>
<li>Categories 0 and 1: cloudy days or dim city light.</li>
<li>Category 2: moderate sun.</li>
<li>Category 3: the most useful here, for a summer or beach day.</li>
<li>Category 4: high mountains, snow or lots of glare off the sea. Not suitable for driving.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Polarised or not</h2>
<p>Polarised lenses cut out glare from water and wet tarmac. For driving and at sea you really notice the difference. On the other hand, some car or phone screens look dark through them.</p>
<h2>Prescription sunglasses</h2>
<p>If you wear glasses, your sunglasses can have your prescription too, varifocals included. It’s more comfortable than wearing clip-ons or swapping glasses all the time.</p>
<h2>Children, all the more so</h2>
<p>A child’s lens is more transparent than an adult’s and lets more radiation through to the retina. For them, proper sunglasses with the CE mark and a UV filter, not toy glasses.</p>
<p>In both our shops we have <a href="/en/brands/">sunglasses from many brands</a>, and we’ll help you choose the category and type of lens that suits you.</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=1000, type="post", path="blog/getting-used-to-varifocals", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/progresivos-como-acostumbrarse/",
     date="2026-11-10", title="Varifocals: how to get used to them in a few days",
     seo_title="Varifocals: How to Get Used to Them in a Few Days",
     desc="How varifocal lenses work, what to do in the first week and why the measurements matter so much. Varilux specialist centre in Albox and Huércal-Overa.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2024/08/salud-visual.jpg", w=720, h=720, alt="Close-up of a green eye"),
     html="""<p>From our early forties, nearly all of us end up holding the phone further away to read it. It’s called presbyopia, and it happens to everyone: the lens inside the eye loses flexibility over the years and finds it harder to focus up close.</p>
<p>Varifocal lenses solve it with a single pair of glasses for distance, the middle range and close up. Many people are wary of the change, so here’s how they work and what to do in the first few days.</p>
<h2>What’s inside a varifocal lens</h2>
<p>The top part is set for distance. As you look down, the prescription changes gradually: the middle covers the distance of the computer or the dashboard, and the bottom is for reading. There’s no line like the old bifocals; the change is smooth.</p>
<p>At the sides of the lens there are areas that look less sharp. That’s what feels strangest at first, so getting used to them is mostly about learning to look through the right spot.</p>
<h2>The first few days</h2>
<ul>
<li>Put them on in the morning and wear them all day. Taking them on and off now and then makes it take longer.</li>
<li>To look to the side, turn your head rather than your eyes, so you keep looking through the centre of the lens.</li>
<li>To read, lower your eyes and keep your head straight.</li>
<li>On stairs and kerbs, at first, tilt your head slightly so you see the step through the distance part.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most people get used to them in one or two weeks. If after that you still feel dizzy, or have to lift your chin to read, come and see us: it’s nearly always a matter of adjusting the frame or checking a measurement, and it can be put right.</p>
<h2>Why the measurements matter so much</h2>
<p>In a varifocal lens, a few millimetres change where each zone falls. The distance between the pupils and the height of each eye have to be measured with the chosen frame already fitted properly to your face.</p>
<p>We have been a Varilux specialist centre since 2000. It’s a title that Essilor, the maker of Varilux lenses, gives to opticians with precise measuring equipment and staff trained to fit varifocals.</p>
<h2>If you spend long hours at the computer</h2>
<p>Varifocals are for everything, but their intermediate zone is narrow. For long days at a screen there are occupational lenses, with much more room for the middle distance and close up, used as a second pair for work.</p>
<p>If you’d like to try them, <a href="/en/stores/">book an appointment in Albox or Huércal-Overa</a> and we’ll take our time over it. There’s more about what we do on our <a href="/en/eye-care/">eye care</a> page.</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=822, type="post", path="blog/digital-eye-strain", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/cansado-ordenador/",
     date="2024-08-08", title="Tired from the computer? Digital eye strain",
     seo_title="Digital Eye Strain from Screens: Causes and Remedies",
     desc="Tired, dry or blurry eyes after hours on the computer or phone. Why it happens, what to do and when to have your eyes tested, explained by Óptica Almería.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2024/08/shutterstock_89179723.jpg", w=1000, h=667, alt="Toddler in a shirt and tie typing on a laptop"),
     html="""<p>TVs, computers, tablets, phones and even digital whiteboards!</p>
<p>One of the most common reasons people come to our shops is the symptoms of digital eye strain, or computer vision syndrome. It’s no surprise, since every day we use digital devices more and for longer. So today we explain what causes it, how to recognise it and what can be done.</p>
<p>Eye strain can have several causes: your vision, the digital screens and the surface of the eye.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eye strain related to your vision.</strong><br>These days we use screens both for work and in our free time, and we use several devices at once, each at a different distance. That’s why it’s so important to have the right prescription at every distance, and a full eye examination including tests of how the eyes converge and focus is also a good idea.</li>
</ul>
<p>We advise changing focus every so often: every 20 minutes, look at something far away for about 20 seconds. Keep good room lighting (natural light if possible), and finally it’s very important to keep a good posture and place the texts or screens you use at the right distance.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eye strain related to the screens.</strong><br>Glare is one of the most common complaints in people with computer vision syndrome. And as we said, we use several devices at once, each with its own contrast, resolution and reflections.<br>Screens give off blue light (between 400 and 500 nm). There are lenses with a blue light filter and anti-reflective coating that cut reflections and give more comfortable contrast. That said, there is currently no evidence that screen light damages the retina: what tires the eyes most is focusing up close for hours and blinking too little.</li>
<li><strong>Eye strain related to the surface of the eye.</strong><br>Our patients very often mention dry eyes after a spell of screen work, especially contact lens wearers. There are several reasons, the main one being that when we look at these devices we blink less often and don’t close the eyelids fully, so our tears don’t spread well and our eyes feel dry.</li>
</ul>
<p>To prevent this feeling, it’s a good idea to take a break every hour at a screen and try to blink more fully and more often. If you already have discomfort, artificial tears relieve it. And if the feeling doesn’t go away, it’s worth having your eyes tested: tiredness often comes from a prescription that is no longer strong enough.</p>
<p>If you often notice these symptoms, book an appointment in Albox or Huércal-Overa and we’ll look into it properly: <a href="/en/stores/">here are our phone numbers</a>. See you soon!</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=641, type="post", path="blog/pterygium-surfers-eye", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/palmeras-los-ojos/",
     date="2017-03-09", title="“Palm trees” in the eyes: what pterygium is",
     seo_title="Pterygium (Surfer’s Eye): What It Is and How to Prevent It",
     desc="Pterygium, called “palm trees” in Spain and surfer’s eye in English, is a benign growth on the eye closely linked to the sun. What it is and how to prevent it.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2017/03/palmeras-e1723176295287.jpg", w=1055, h=978, alt="Close-up of an eye with a sunflower drawn on the iris"),
     html="""<h3>Benign eye conditions</h3>
<p>Good morning and happy Thursday, everyone!</p>
<p>Many of you will never have heard of “palm trees” in the eyes, as they are called in Spain. Some of you will have, and others may have already asked me what they are and how they can be cured, because it’s a common problem, especially in very sunny places like ours. Today I’ll tell you about it: pterygium, known in English as surfer’s eye.</p>
<p>I’ll start by saying that pterygium isn’t a disease but an abnormal, benign growth of the conjunctival tissue. It looks like a small raised membrane that usually appears on the side nearest the nose and grows towards the cornea. In most cases it stays stable and doesn’t affect vision, but sometimes it keeps growing over the cornea, changes its shape and makes eyesight worse.</p>
<p>Pterygium is closely linked to ultraviolet radiation from the sun. That’s why it’s fairly common in Almería, especially in people who have spent a lot of time out in the sun.</p>
<p>Another eye condition is pinguecula: a small yellowish bump or spot on the conjunctiva that you can see with the naked eye but which, unlike pterygium, doesn’t reach the cornea. Pinguecula stays on the white of the eye; pterygium grows over onto the clear front of the eye.</p>
<p>Although having “palm trees” isn’t serious, it can cause very annoying symptoms such as dry eye, because the raised tissue breaks up the tear film and stops it spreading properly.</p>
<p>When pterygium spreads into the field of vision it is operated on. In mild cases surgery isn’t usually advised, because it can come back afterwards, although with today’s techniques that happens far less than it used to. It is always the ophthalmologist’s decision.</p>
<p>To help with the discomfort we should use specific eyelid cleaning and artificial tears to moisturise and lubricate the eye; that’s the best way to relieve the symptoms.</p>
<p>Finally, as always, the best remedy is prevention! It’s very important to wear <a href="/en/blog/choosing-sunglasses/">approved sunglasses with the CE mark and a UV filter</a> to protect your eyes… and they look great too!</p>
<p>Until the next post!</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=569, type="post", path="blog/silmo-paris", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/me-enamore-paris/",
     date="2016-12-13", title="I fell in love in Paris: my visit to Silmo",
     seo_title="Silmo Paris: the Eyewear Fair I Fell in Love With",
     desc="Carmen, from Óptica Almería, tells the story of her visit to Silmo in Paris, one of Europe’s biggest eyewear fairs, and what she brought back to the shop.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2016/12/paris.jpg", w=720, h=720, alt="The Eiffel Tower reflected in a pair of sunglasses"),
     html="""<p>Good morning, everyone! Or rather, bonjour!</p>
<p>As the title gives away, today I’m telling you about the crushes I had in Paris.</p>
<p>While looking up optical events and fairs online, I decided to pack my bags and head to Paris to visit one of the most important fairs in Europe, Silmo. It’s where the coveted design awards are handed out, where every label presents its latest collections and where young brands in the sector make themselves known. And of course, being the “glasses addict” that I am, I couldn’t miss it…</p>
<p>I knew the fair was a big deal and had more than 1,300 brands, but what I found there was so much more.</p>
<p>At every step there was a pair of glasses that won me over. I was going crazy with so many colours and shapes, I didn’t know where to start looking! There were 12 different areas with around 900 stands hiding an endless number of frames.</p>
<p>More than a fair, for me it was an adventure. Each stand was completely different from the next, whether in colour or style… But every single one of them was special, with that something that makes you want it right there and then. That’s why I couldn’t resist and decided to bring a little piece of it back to the shop.</p>
<p>A word to the wise!</p>
<p>If you love glasses, you can’t miss this event… And if you don’t want to travel that far… Don’t worry, just drop in at <a href="/en/brands/">our shops</a> and you can fall in love at first sight too!</p>
<p>PS: I dedicate this post to my favourite glasses adviser! Anie</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=635, type="post", path="blog/lenses-for-screens", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/lentes-protectoras/",
     date="2016-12-12", title="Lenses for screens and tips to avoid tired eyes",
     seo_title="Lenses for Screens and Tips to Avoid Tired Eyes",
     desc="If you spend hours on the computer or phone, there are lenses designed for that. What Essilor’s Eyezen lenses are, and four simple habits to keep your eyes from tiring.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2016/12/izen.jpg", w=720, h=720, alt="Young woman with glasses resting her chin on her hand"),
     html="""<p>If your eyesight isn’t your best friend, or is even turning into your worst enemy… don’t lose heart!</p>
<p>Essilor, the maker of Varilux lenses, has lenses called Eyezen™ designed for people aged 20 to 45 who spend many hours with screens. They work for people who are short-sighted, long-sighted or astigmatic, and also for those who see well but end the day with tired eyes.</p>
<p>We all know our daily lives are full of computers, phones, TVs, tablets… which are essential to us and above all a great help, but which hide some big problems: from sore eyes to eye fatigue, itchy eyes, blurred vision and so on.</p>
<p>These lenses aim to reduce these problems as much as possible, so that people who need to spend hours at a computer every day don’t suffer from poor vision.</p>
<p>As well as wearing the right lenses, these habits help look after your eyes day to day:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep your distance when reading.</strong> The closer we are to the screen or the book, the harder the eye has to work to focus on the text.</li>
<li><strong>Lighting.</strong> When we use our eyes for a good while, ideally there should be a good light source so they don’t get tired.</li>
<li><strong>Step away from the screen now and then.</strong> Spending many hours in a row in front of a computer isn’t advisable, not only for your eyes but also to avoid back and neck aches. Every 20 minutes, look at something far away for about 20 seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Sit at the right distance.</strong> The ideal minimum distance is about the length of your arm.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you’d like to know whether these lenses suit you, <a href="/en/eye-care/">come in for an eye test</a> in Albox or Huércal-Overa.</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=507, type="post", path="blog/addicted-to-glasses", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/adicta-las-gafas/",
     date="2016-10-27", title="Addicted to glasses",
     seo_title="Addicted to Glasses: Carmen’s Blog",
     desc="I’m Carmen, from the family that founded Óptica Almería. Here I share trends, new arrivals and the glasses I love most.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2016/10/adicta-gafas-e1723176083340.jpg", w=1080, h=977, alt="Woman wearing pink octagonal sunglasses and a denim jacket"),
     html="""<p>Hello, world! My name is Carmen and I’m <strong>addicted to glasses!</strong></p>
<p>I’ll be the one bringing you the trends and new arrivals of my favourite accessory!</p>
<p>I’ll start this blog by explaining that my addiction began at an early age, since my family founded Óptica Almería, so ever since I was a little girl I’ve been surrounded by glasses of every colour and size.</p>
<p>I remember perfectly coming home from school and trying on, again and again, the frame I would wear on the day I finally needed glasses… I wanted to wear glasses so badly that once my mum’s sunglasses stopped being enormous on me, there was no turning back! Since then I can’t live without them.</p>
<p>Today, not only can I not control my habit, it’s getting worse! I love discovering new materials, original designs and the coolest brands! Every day I look for the latest from my favourite labels, and whenever I can I visit the most important trade fairs, so my habit and my collection keep getting bigger!</p>
<p>I created this blog because I believe the world is full of people who, like me, aren’t happy with just any pair of glasses: people who want something different, a special little treat!</p>
<p>I hope you find my little articles interesting and, of course, my latest discoveries.</p>
<p>See you soon, fellow addicts!</p>
<p>PS: The glasses in my photo are Prada. You can see the brands we stock in the shop today <a href="/en/brands/">here</a>.</p>
<p><img src="https://www.opticalmeria.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/adicta-gafas.jpg" alt="Carmen, from Óptica Almería, wearing Prada glasses" loading="lazy"></p>"""),

dict(wp_id=518, type="post", path="blog/contact-lens-care-tips", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/7-consejos-cuidar-tus-lentes-contacto/",
     date="2016-10-27", title="7 tips for looking after your contact lenses",
     seo_title="7 Tips for Looking After Your Contact Lenses",
     desc="Clean hands, the right solution and sticking to the replacement date: seven tips from Óptica Almería for wearing contact lenses without discomfort or infections.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2016/10/lentillas2.jpg", w=720, h=720, alt="Woman putting in a contact lens"),
     html="""<p>People often come to our shops with problems with their contact lenses. Although there are many reasons why you might not “tolerate” your lenses, one of the most common is not looking after them properly.</p>
<p>So in this post we’re giving you <strong>7 tips</strong> so that looking after your lenses is never a problem!</p>
<ul>
<li>One of the most important things when wearing contact lenses is <strong>hygiene</strong>, so every time you handle your lenses wash your hands to avoid contaminating them. Dry your hands with materials that don’t leave fluff, and avoid creams or products that can leave grease and contaminate the lenses.</li>
<li>It’s essential to use the right <strong>care products</strong> for you. Depending on your needs, your type of lens and your tears, you’ll need one kind of care or another. Visit us at either of our shops and we’ll recommend the ones that suit you best.</li>
<li>You should <strong>rinse</strong> your contact lenses before putting them in and after taking them out, even the first time you open the pack. <strong>Never</strong> rinse contact lenses with <strong>tap water</strong>, because it can carry microorganisms that infect the eye. For the same reason, take them out for the shower, the pool or the sea.</li>
<li>If you need a specific <strong>cleaner</strong> for your contact lenses, make sure you follow each product’s instructions carefully. Rub the lens gently with your fingertips.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Lens <strong>cases</strong> can build up a large number of bacteria, so don’t leave solution in them when there are no lenses inside. They should also be replaced regularly, at least every three months.</li>
<li><strong>Change the solution</strong> every time you store your lenses. Make sure the lenses are well covered. When lenses dry out they get damaged more easily and become less comfortable.</li>
<li>Don’t stretch the life of your lenses. A monthly lens doesn’t last 30 uses, but 30 days from when you take it out of its pack. Even if you look after them well, don’t use them past their <strong>replacement date</strong>: lenses deteriorate and dry out over time, however much or little you’ve worn them. Replace your contact lenses as your optometrist advises.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have any questions about looking after your lenses, as well as at <a href="/en/stores/">either of our shops</a> you can also get in touch by email at info@opticalmeria.com, on Facebook or on Instagram. Don’t hesitate to ask!</p>
<p>We hope this post has been helpful!</p>"""),

dict(wp_id=220, type="post", path="blog/welcome", es_url="https://www.opticalmeria.com/bienvenidos-al-blog-de-optica-almeria/",
     date="2016-06-14", title="Welcome to the Óptica Almería blog",
     seo_title="Welcome to the Óptica Almería Blog",
     desc="The blog of Óptica Almería, opticians and hearing care specialists in Albox and Huércal-Overa: eye health, contact lenses, glasses and hearing explained without jargon.",
     image=dict(src=WP + "2016/06/bienvenidos.jpg", w=720, h=720, alt="Óptica Almería logo"),
     html="""<p>Welcome to the Óptica Almería blog.</p>
<p>A blog dedicated to <strong>your eyes</strong>, focused on <strong>your vision</strong>.</p>
<p>At Óptica Almería we believe in a personal touch, and talking to our customers is essential for that. That’s why we created this space, so you can find all the information about your vision and we can respond to your needs. As well as on this blog, you can find us on Facebook and Instagram, where we share content every day that may interest you. You can follow us here:</p>
<p><a href="https://www.facebook.com/opticalmeria/">https://www.facebook.com/opticalmeria/</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.instagram.com/opticalmeria/">https://www.instagram.com/opticalmeria/</a></p>
<p>In this section we’ll talk about the latest advances in vision and the questions we are often asked in our shops. The Óptica Almería team will cover the topics that interest you most. We are grateful for your help, comments and suggestions so we can keep improving our customer care and offer you a quality service.</p>
<p>Many thanks from the whole <strong>Óptica Almería</strong> team.</p>
<p>See you soon!</p>"""),

